<p>The only major complaint I have is that this feels like it ends just as it's getting started. Harkand's death feels decidedly abrupt and anticlimatic; that's not inherently a bad thing, but as the conclusion to your article it didn't work for me. I'm not all that curious about where they take people, simply because I don't have much of a reference point for that aspect of the horror. Stuff like 106's pocket dimension and the lake's ulterior dimension worked because we were provided certain context that allowed our minds to fill in the blanks; here, there's nothing, so it felt empty and kind of a useless plot point.</p>
